      "generated_answer": "In the scenario described, the player attempting a Lateral Group Transfer must follow the rules defined in 17.8 of the UF RuleBook updated.pdf. According to [17.8], a Lateral Group Transfer requires a player to announce the intention to perform a transfer and place a Movement card sideways, which implies the transfer is to be made directly opposite an opposing group. The rule states, \"An unpinned man in a group which is neither encircled, nor on Wire/Minefield, may transfer from one group to an adjacent friendly group with the same range number, by placing a Movement card in the discard tray for each man he wishes to transfer as the sole action of the sending group for that turn.\" This means the Movement card must be placed in a manner indicating the transfer directly opposite an opposing group.\n\nGiven the rule, the American player's attempt to perform a Lateral Group Transfer on Group C would be illegal if the German player does not have a corresponding group at the identity chit C. The transfer must be directly opposite an opposing group as per the first sentence of Rule 17.8. The German player's only groups are at identity chit A and B, which means the American player cannot perform a Lateral Group Transfer to C since there is no opposing group directly opposite at the identity chit C.\n\nIf the player does not make the announcement at the time of playing the Movement card sideways, the attempt cannot be retroactively changed into a Lateral Group Transfer in future turns using the same Movement card (Rule 17.8 implies this by stating the transfer must be announced and performed in the turn in which the Movement card is played).\n\nAs for changing one's mind after declaring the intention to perform a Lateral Group Transfer, there is no specific rule that addresses this scenario. However, by the nature of the rules and the mechanics of the game, once the intention is declared, the player would need to proceed with the transfer or risk penalties or disqualification for failing to follow through as intended by the rules. \n\nThus, the correct action for the American player is to not perform the Lateral Group Transfer since the conditions are not met (no corresponding German group at identity chit C). The movement card should be treated as a regular movement or an attempt to change terrain, as allowed by the card and the game's rules, but it cannot serve as a Lateral Group Transfer under the circumstances provided.\n\nThis text has changed across versions. Would you like to know how it worked in a specific version, or how it has evolved over time?",
      "ground_truth_answer": "In the General Vol 21 Number 1 scenario A series replay, on turn 5 the American plays a sideways movement card on his group C \"as a Lateral Group Transfer\". But given the German player only has groups at identity chit A and B isn't this illegal? Rule 17.7 first sentence says \"... directly opposite an opposing group ...\"\n\nNo, it says \"A group capable of movement can change its group identity and thereby its position directly opposing an enemy group....\"  All it is saying if you move group from B to C, the directly opposing group would now be C--if it exists.  If the opponent  does not have a Group C, then there would be no directly opposing group to your Group C.  (Just like there would be if each side set up with a different number of groups at start.)\n\nIt is not stating a requirement, but a change of status of \"directly opposing\" groups.\n\nThe rule states the player announces the intention to perform a lateral group transfer so presumably if the player doesn't make the announcement at the time of playing the movement card sideways he can't subsequently do so on a future turn (using that same played movement card) even if the necessary conditions still exist to make such a move? \n\nCorrect, if the intention to change a group's ID is not made when playing the movement cards, when group enters terrain, the same Group ID remains.  Very common if you want to get into better terrain.\n\nWhere a game is played without aid of voice or text chat I guess you might agree that a lateral group transfer announcement was accompanied by putting down the new group identity chit in the adjacent column and taken away if the attempt failed.\n\nA common technique in ftf play is to place the new ID on the Movement card until new terrain is accepted or rejected (which would cancel the transfer).\n\nI wonder whether having declared the intention to lateral group transfer a player can change their mind? \n\nI would say no per errata on Rule 5:  Movement cards not played directly to the discard pile cannot voluntarily be cancelled.  (Just like you can't rotate a Movement card from advancing to sideways because you change your mind about advancing.)\n\nEdit: Looking through past posts I see I have broached this issue before! See https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/1342845/article/35597336#35597336 where no conclusion was reached :)\n\nNo big deal, that thread started 11 years ago and you added your question 5 years after that.",
